systemd: bound the no-close_range fd loop by /proc/self/fd

0009 routes the missing close_range() to close_all_fds_frugal(). The port still
did not run a single unit -- only the errno moved, from ENOSYS to EPERM:

  Failed to close unwanted file descriptors: Operation not permitted
  Failed at step FDS spawning .../getty: Operation not permitted

The EPERM is synthetic. It is close_all_fds_frugal()'s own refusal:

        if (max_fd > MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT)
                return log_debug_errno(SYNTHETIC_ERRNO(EPERM),
                                       "Refusing to loop over %d potential fds.", max_fd);

It is emitted at debug level, and the kernel cmdline pins systemd.log_level=info
(which outranks system.conf's LogLevel=), so nothing about it reaches the log.
Sending SIGRTMIN+22 to PID 1 from boot.postBootCommands raises the level at
runtime and the line appears, dandelion stage-2 boot of 2026-08-07:

  [ 57.428940] dhcpcd.service: Refusing to loop over 2147483583 potential fds.
  [ 57.429053] dhcpcd.service: Failed to close unwanted file descriptors:
               Operation not permitted
  [ 57.429150] dhcpcd.service: Failed at step FDS spawning
               .../migrate-dhcpcd: Operation not permitted

118 FDS failures in that boot; the 69 that happen after the signal each carry a
refusal line ahead of them, same PID, under a millisecond apart. 1:1, no
exceptions. The 49 before the signal are the same failure with the explanation
suppressed.

Where 2147483583 comes from, every value read off the device:

  main.c:1358 bump_file_max_and_nr_open()  writes fs/nr_open to the kernel max
                                           -> /proc/sys/fs/nr_open  2147483584
  main.c:1408 bump_rlimit_nofile()         sets PID 1's RLIMIT_NOFILE cur AND
                                           max to read_nr_open()
                                           -> /proc/1/limits  2147483584/2147483584
  get_max_fd()                             MAX(cur, max) - 1
                                           -> 2147483583, just under INT_MAX so
                                              the saturation branch never fires
  MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT                        1048576

exec-invoke.c runs setrlimit_closest_all() at line 5929, after close_remaining_fds()
at 5298, so the forked child is still carrying PID 1's inherited rlimits when it
reaches the FDS step. get_max_fd() therefore measures what RLIMIT_NOFILE permits,
not what is open. On a kernel with close_range() this never matters, because
frugal is only the OOM and signal-handler path; without it, it is the only path,
and it refuses on every single spawn.

No descriptor is actually failing to close. PID 1's fd table, dumped twice at
60s and 120s (50 then 45 entries, highest fd 53), holds only /dev/null, sockets,
pipes, timerfd/eventpoll/inotify/signalfd anon_inodes, /dev/kmsg,
/proc/1/mountinfo, /proc/swaps, /sys/fs/cgroup and the systemd-executor binary.
No vendor device node, so no f_op->flush() has any way to return EPERM.

The fix is upstream's own, from the comment above the constant:

  /* The maximum number of iterations in the loop to close descriptors in the
   * fallback case when /proc/self/fd/ is inaccessible. */
  #define MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT (1024*1024)

The /proc/self/fd walk was the primary fallback until close_range() replaced it,
and MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT exists to bound the case where that walk is unavailable.
So reinstate the walk as close_all_fds_no_close_range() and point the two ENOSYS
sites at it. It bounds the loop by what is open (53 here) rather than by the
rlimit (2147483583), which is 40 million times fewer iterations than frugal would
have refused to do.

close_all_fds_frugal() is left exactly as it is and stays the last resort, both
when /proc/self/fd cannot be read and for close_all_fds_without_malloc(), whose
only caller passes (NULL, 0) from signal handler context -- opendir() allocates,
so that path must not use the new code. Its refusal is deliberate there.

The directory is read to completion before anything is closed; closing during
iteration would mutate the directory being read. The DIR's own fd is often the
highest one, and the cleanup attribute closes it before the loop starts, so the
loop simply sees EBADF for it, which close_all_fds_frugal() already treats as
"nothing there".

close_range() is genuinely absent, not merely erroring. A static aarch64-musl
probe calling syscall(436, ...) three ways on the device:

  close_range(3, 5, 0)           = -1 errno=38 (Function not implemented)
  close_range(100, 200, 0)       = -1 errno=38 (Function not implemented)
  close_range(3, 2147483647, 0)  = -1 errno=38 (Function not implemented)

--- a/src/basic/fd-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/fd-util.c
@@ -288,6 +288,61 @@
         return r;
 }
 
+/* Returns the highest fd currently open in this process, or a negative errno if /proc/self/fd is not
+ * readable. Unlike get_max_fd() this reports what is actually open rather than what RLIMIT_NOFILE would
+ * permit, so the bound stays small even when the rlimit is raised to fs.nr_open. */
+static int highest_open_fd(void) {
+        _cleanup_closedir_ DIR *d = NULL;
+        int max_fd = 2;
+
+        d = opendir("/proc/self/fd");
+        if (!d)
+                return -errno;
+
+        /* Read the directory to completion before closing anything: closing while iterating would mutate
+         * the very directory being read. */
+        FOREACH_DIRENT(de, d, return -errno) {
+                int fd;
+
+                if (safe_atoi(de->d_name, &fd) < 0)
+                        continue;
+
+                if (fd > max_fd)
+                        max_fd = fd;
+        }
+
+        return max_fd;
+}
+
+static int close_all_fds_no_close_range(const int except[], size_t n_except) {
+        int max_fd, r = 0;
+
+        assert(except || n_except == 0);
+
+        /* Fallback for kernels without close_range(). close_all_fds_frugal() would refuse outright once
+         * RLIMIT_NOFILE exceeds MAX_FD_LOOP_LIMIT, so bound the loop by /proc/self/fd instead and keep
+         * frugal as the last resort for when /proc is not there. */
+
+        max_fd = highest_open_fd();
+        if (max_fd < 0)
+                return close_all_fds_frugal(except, n_except);
+
+        /* The DIR's own fd may well be the highest one; the cleanup attribute closed it above, so the loop
+         * just sees EBADF for it. */
+        for (int fd = 3; fd <= max_fd; fd++) {
+                int q;
+
+                if (fd_in_set(fd, except, n_except))
+                        continue;
+
+                q = close_nointr(fd);
+                if (q != -EBADF)
+                        RET_GATHER(r, q);
+        }
+
+        return r;
+}
+
 static int close_all_fds_special_case(const int except[], size_t n_except) {
         assert(n_except == 0 || except);
 
@@ -396,7 +451,7 @@
                 /* Close everything between the start and end fds (both of which shall stay open) */
                 if (close_range(start + 1, end - 1, 0) < 0) {
                         if (errno == ENOSYS)
-                                return close_all_fds_frugal(except, n_except);
+                                return close_all_fds_no_close_range(except, n_except);
                         return -errno;
                 }
         }
@@ -408,7 +463,7 @@
 
         if (close_range(sorted[n_sorted-1] + 1, INT_MAX, 0) < 0) {
                 if (errno == ENOSYS)
-                        return close_all_fds_frugal(except, n_except);
+                        return close_all_fds_no_close_range(except, n_except);
                 return -errno;
         }
 
